[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The fabric is healthy; leaf_router_0_1’s uplinks are up and routing is functioning end-to-end. Evidence:
- End-to-end reachability works: pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 pings succeed with 0% loss and ~1.4–1.5 ms RTT.
- Physical links are up: ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on leaf_router_0_1 eth0 and eth1, and on the facing spine ports (spine_router_0_0 eth2, spine_router_0_1 eth2).
- Control-plane is established: on leaf_router_0_1, both BGP sessions to the spines (neighbors 172.16.0.6 and 172.16.0.10) are Established, each receiving one prefix. On leaf_router_0_1 the RIB shows a BGP route to 10.0.0.0/24 via eth1, and on leaf_router_0_0 a BGP route to 10.0.1.0/24 via eth1. This is consistent with both uplinks being operational and ECMP-capable.
Mechanism: If both uplinks were down, BGP adjacencies would be Idle/Down and remote rack prefixes would vanish from the RIB/FIB, causing ping failure. Instead, adjacencies are up and remote prefixes are installed, so traffic forwards normally.
Localization: Verified on leaf_router_0_1 (eth0/eth1, BGP) and matching spine interfaces.
Root cause: No current fault; the reported outage was transient or a misdiagnosis.
Corrective fix: No action required. If intermittent issues recur, monitor for interface flaps and BGP resets (enable logging, consider BFD), but as of now the rack’s uplinks are up and service is restored. 
=== END ===